{"parentUuid":"a4825dee-fff5-404e-a83e-b5be19eb096b","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"e19bdc88-8720-4a18-bfda-8b8705671811","agentId":"a0c9a4cf695e65888","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GXsymSbpKAudhPtDSw3a62","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"image","source":{"type":"base64","data":"","media_type":"image/png"}}]}]},"uuid":"26890248-0037-427c-8ae3-63655d72c2e2","timestamp":"2026-06-10T08:33:47.136Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"a4825dee-fff5-404e-a83e-b5be19eb096b","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/dmitrymanning-coe/Documents/Research/FRA/fra_proj/experiments/fra_win","sessionId":"8a660228-bde0-4e60-bfe2-aafb33fe0b4d","version":"2.1.170","gitBranch":"autoresearch/fra-behavioral-win","slug":"moonlit-churning-gem"}
{"parentUuid":"26890248-0037-427c-8ae3-63655d72c2e2","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a0c9a4cf695e65888","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_01BEPr9vxmC3Zhtums8DTSN8","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Here's my read of this figure, from the figure alone.\n\n**1. One-sentence claim**\nFRA-QK (a bilinear, content-addressed editing method) can suppress a single token's induction behavior with roughly 15× less collateral damage to the model's behavior on normal text than the fair linear baseline (ActAdd), at matched suppression strength.\n\n**2. Axes**\n- Left panel x-axis: \"induction suppression (1 − P/base) → stronger\" — how much the targeted induction association is knocked down (0 = no effect, 1 = fully suppressed). Right is good (stronger intended effect).\n- Left panel y-axis: \"held-out collateral: KL on normal text (nats)\", log scale — how much the edit disturbs the model on unrelated/held-out text. Down is good (less collateral). So the ideal point is bottom-right: strong on-target suppression, low off-target damage.\n- Right panel y-axis: \"held-out collateral at 50% suppression (nats)\", linear — collateral cost at a fixed, matched suppression level (50%). Down/lower bars are good. x-axis is just the four methods (categorical).\n\n**3. Methods compared and winner**\nThree methods (left panel legend): FRA-QK (bilinear, content-addressed), ActAdd induction-gated (\"fair linear\"), and ActAdd cue-identity (linear). FRA-QK wins clearly: its trajectory sits as a band far below the two ActAdd variants across the whole suppression range (roughly an order of magnitude lower KL). In the right panel, at matched 50% suppression, FRA-QK content-addressed = 0.18 nats vs ActAdd induction-gated = 2.66 and ActAdd cue-identity = 3.27. The title quantifies it as ~15× better than the \"fair baseline\" (2.66 / 0.18 ≈ 14.8×).\n\n**4. What the right panel adds / objection pre-empted**\nThe right panel does two things. First, it controls for suppression strength: the left-panel curves span different x-positions, so a skeptic could argue FRA only looks better because it suppresses less. The right panel reads off collateral at a *fixed* 50% suppression, making it an apples-to-apples comparison with error bars. Second, and more pointedly, it adds a fourth bar — \"FRA-QK (edge-restricted)\" at 0.14 nats — that is absent from the left panel. The subtitle says \"content-addressed ≈ edge-restricted (support not rigged).\" This pre-empts the objection that FRA's advantage comes from cherry-picking *which* attention edges/positions it's allowed to touch (a rigged support set). Showing that a restricted-support variant gives essentially the same low collateral (0.14 vs 0.18) argues the win is real, not an artifact of a privileged edit support.\n\n**5. Things that are confusing / unexplained from the figure alone**\n- \"FRA-QK,\" \"ActAdd,\" \"content-addressed,\" \"edge-restricted,\" \"induction-gated,\" and \"cue-identity\" are all undefined here — I'm inferring meanings. ActAdd I recognize as activation-addition steering; the rest I'm guessing at.\n- \"n=4 cues\" and the multiple faint lines: the left panel clearly has many more than 4 trajectories per method (looks like ~8+ lines each, with light/dark shading), so what each line represents (cues? seeds? layers? tokens?) and what the faded vs solid lines mean is not explained.\n- \"near-faithful scale c≈2\" in the title is unexplained — presumably an edit-strength scaling factor, but its role isn't clear, and it's odd that the headline single-token claim is tied to one specific c.\n- The right panel only shows FRA-QK at one operating point but the left shows a trajectory; \"50% suppression\" is the chosen matching point but why 50% (vs the c≈2 in the left title) isn't reconciled.\n- The error bars on the ActAdd bars are very large (induction-gated spans ~1.3–4.0; cue-identity ~1.5–5.1) and asymmetric/clipped — so the \"~15×\" point estimate hides a lot of variance, and the two ActAdd bars overlap each other heavily. The FRA error bars are essentially invisible at this scale, which is favorable but unquantified.\n- \"fair linear\" vs \"(linear)\" labeling implies one ActAdd is a deliberately matched/fair control and the other isn't, but what makes one \"fair\" isn't stated.\n- Units are given (nats) and that's good; but KL \"on normal text\" — which text distribution, and KL between what and what (edited vs base model?) — is left implicit.\n\nFile analyzed: `/Users/dmitrymanning-coe/Documents/Research/FRA/fra_proj/experiments/fra_win/figures/fig1_final.png`"}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":813,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8261,"cache_read_input_tokens":9473,"output_tokens":1602,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":8261},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":813,"output_tokens":1602,"cache_read_input_tokens":9473,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8261,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":8261,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011CbuGfF2MADMzJxSWfh6CS","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"c6ca5646-485c-4eda-8dfa-efaa863d1b68","timestamp":"2026-06-10T08:34:21.725Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/dmitrymanning-coe/Documents/Research/FRA/fra_proj/experiments/fra_win","sessionId":"8a660228-bde0-4e60-bfe2-aafb33fe0b4d","version":"2.1.170","gitBranch":"autoresearch/fra-behavioral-win","slug":"moonlit-churning-gem"}
